{"_type":"item","id":"01a15f3389e574e8891adee29dd8ea29","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Private Opportunity Engine: evidence-gated property and market research prototype (deferred)","description":"Status: DEFERRED by the owner (2026-10-21). Nothing has been built.\n\nSource: ~/Downloads/private_opportunity_engine_thought_paper.md (v1.0, 2026-10-19), on the owner's Linux box.\n\n## The idea\nBuild a private engine, not a product, to find individual assets or underserved local markets that the market has mispriced. It pursues them lawfully and learns from realized results. It combines three earlier ideas:\n- CivicGraph: joining public geographic and administrative data.\n- Morpheus: a narrow set of testable signals of local change.\n- Capital Lab: a ledger of hypotheses, decisions and outcomes.\n\nH1: calibrated valuation of public records, plus listings, plus human diligence beats simple comparable sales after costs.\nH0: it doesn't, and the work stops. A well-supported \"no\" counts as success.\n\nStarting market: King County, as a test bed. Scenarios:\n- S1: a listed property priced below a cautious comparable-sales estimate. This is the first MVP.\n- S4: a capped, low-capital test of an underserved local market. It runs in parallel and is the fallback.\n- S2, S3 and S5 come later.\n\nEach stage has a stop gate: data (weeks 1–4), model (5–8), discovery (9–12), then a 90–180-day shadow portfolio.\n\n## Settled in discussion (2026-10-19 to 2026-10-21)\n- Assessor data is fine for private analysis. The assessor's RCW 42.56.070(9) warning targets lists of individuals used for unsolicited commercial contact, which is out of scope. The owner has used county sales records in a past research project.\n- Current listings are captured through normal site interfaces, by a human or by an LLM under human supervision, at normal pace. There is no bulk scraping. Prefer a human-driven browser session where a site's terms ban automated agents.\n- Collection-bias guard: record the exact search criteria and everything each search returned, not only the listings that looked interesting.\n- Backtest first. That needs no live listings.\n  - Valuation is trained on the assessor sales history. The tables refresh weekly, and a sale appears a few weeks after it closes.\n  - The S1 discount test uses a random sample of past sales. For each one, read the asking-price history on its property page, with the Internet Archive as a spot check.\n  - Market-condition inputs come from the Redfin Data Center, Zillow Research and Realtor.com research data (free, market-level).\n- APIs:\n  - Zillow's public API ended in 2021. Its successor, Bridge Interactive, is only for MLS-affiliated brokers (reportedly about $500/month and up).\n  - Redfin has no public API.\n  - Cheap \"Zillow APIs\" are third-party scrapers, so avoid them.\n  - Licensed options: RentCast (live listings from non-MLS sources), ATTOM (30-day trial), Cotality (formerly CoreLogic).\n- MLS: NWMLS licenses data only to members, for about $30/month. Eastern Washington has separate MLSs. Get access through a broker partner, and only after the model gate passes.\n\n## Open when resumed\n- Run the paper's own \"$0 spreadsheet plus saved searches plus brokers\" baseline by hand as the H0 control, before building infrastructure.\n- Consider S4 as the first bet, not just the fallback: low capital, fast feedback, proprietary customer data, no listing-rights dependency.\n- Trim the Sprint 0–3 stack to one Python and DuckDB app. Add PostGIS, an API service and agents only after the model gate passes.\n\nRelated: plugins discussion 01a072babbe575d09e57361c9b9a4225 (Council plugin; it cites the Capital Lab paper as one domain example).","created_at":"2026-10-21T16:02:55","updated_at":"2026-10-21T16:02:55","priority":4,"tags":["capital-lab","deferred","opportunity-engine","thought-paper"]}
